{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790419088,"nonce":"NfVkoHgNj0jES6XCobpGuVCCX5I=","title":"Lua tokenize with the LLVM tier runs 2 to 3% slower since the JIT range change","message":"On the NUC (release, llvm-backend, ZYLUA_LLVM=1, warm-up on, pinned to P-cores, perf stat -r 10 three times interleaved), benchmarks/tokenize.lua takes 206 ms wall at 063d2ae4 and 212 to 214 ms at d140ff3226d98f216597b51da713bc27922e61d6, cycles 1.54 to 1.58 G against 1.59 to 1.62 G. At the Cranelift tier with warm-up off the two are level (cycles 786 to 810 M both). With the LLVM tier the user instruction count also rises about 2.5%, so the extra time is likely more work (when code is compiled or installed relative to the loop) rather than code placement alone. The other Lua and Python micro benchmarks and the official files are within their run-to-run spread.\n\nHistory: the first placement of that commit (pieces from the bottom of the range up) cost tokenize about 3% at the Cranelift tier as well, with about 30% more instruction-TLB page walks; placing segments from the top down, as the system places separate mappings, removed that. Next: compare ZYNTAX_OSR_TRACE / lazy traces for the two binaries to see which compiles land at different times.","files":null}]}